--- What happens to a view when its trigger is retracted, and why a cascade
--- depends on the answer.
---
--- DR-0043 pins a rule firing to its trigger's VALUES: matching gates admission
--- only, so a firing whose trigger is consumed still completes. That decision
--- exists to stop a continuation being stranded -- the ch. 15 stall, where
--- work in flight could never settle because the fact that admitted it had
--- gone. A view has no continuations: it enqueues nothing and waits for
--- nothing. So pinning's reason does not reach it, and the opposite rule is
--- available: a view is a maintained statement about its subject, and when the
--- subject is withdrawn the statement is withdrawn with it, derivation and all.
---
--- THE CASCADE IS WHAT MAKES THIS LOAD-BEARING rather than tidy. A recorded
--- fact's key is `record_fact_key`, a hash of the whole value except when the
--- value carries a top-level `number` field. A view's output usually has no
--- such field, so every re-derivation mints a NEW key, hence a new firing
--- identity, hence a DISTINCT downstream firing. The downstream firing is
--- therefore modeled as keyed by the upstream VALUE, which is exactly what the
--- content-hashed key makes it.
---
--- Given that, if a view survived its trigger's retraction, an upstream
--- supersede would leave the old downstream firing deriving from its stale
--- pinned value WHILE a new firing derives from the new one. Supersede's "one
--- current value per firing" would still hold at each firing and yet the
--- number of firings would grow with every upstream move -- the accumulation
--- supersede exists to prevent, reappearing one level down. The PINNED module
--- below is that counterfactual, and it reaches exactly that state.
---
--- Properties [models/maude/tests/view-retraction.maude]:
---   coverage: a retracted trigger ends the view; after an upstream supersede
---     the downstream derivation is the NEW one; the superseded firing is
---     recorded as ended rather than silently dropped.
---   invariants: an ended view never holds a derivation; a cascade never holds
---     two downstream derivations at once.
---   bite: the PINNED module keeps the firing alive across the retraction and
---     reaches two downstream derivations, which is the accumulation the
---     ruling prevents.

fmod VIEW-RETRACTION-STATE is
  protecting BOOL .

  sorts Cnt Fid Cfg .

  op z : -> Cnt [ctor] .
  op s : Cnt -> Cnt [ctor] .
  op lt : Cnt Cnt -> Bool .
  vars A B : Cnt .
  eq lt(z, s(A)) = true .
  eq lt(A, z) = false .
  eq lt(s(A), s(B)) = lt(A, B) .

  op v1 : -> Fid [ctor] .

  op none : -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op __ : Cfg Cfg -> Cfg [ctor assoc comm id: none] .

  op clk : Cnt -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op fuel : Cnt -> Cfg [ctor] .

  --- The single-view case: a trigger that may be withdrawn under the view.
  op trigPresent : Fid -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op trigRetracted : Fid -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op viewFresh : Fid -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op viewLive : Fid -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op viewEnded : Fid -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op current : Fid Cnt -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op qset : Cnt -> Cfg [ctor] .

  --- The cascade: an upstream view's current value, and the downstream firing
  --- keyed by that value. Keyed by the VALUE because the content-hashed fact
  --- key makes each upstream derivation a distinct downstream admission.
  op up : Cnt -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op downFresh : Cnt -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op downLive : Cnt -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op downEnded : Cnt -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op downCurrent : Cnt Cnt -> Cfg [ctor] .

  op initSingle : -> Cfg .
  eq initSingle = clk(z) qset(s(z)) trigPresent(v1) viewFresh(v1) .

  op initCascade : -> Cfg .
  eq initCascade = clk(z) fuel(s(s(z))) up(z) downFresh(z) .

  op initPinned : -> Cfg .
  eq initPinned = clk(z) fuel(s(s(z))) up(z) downFresh(z) .
endfm

mod WHIPPLESCRIPT-VIEW-RETRACTION is
  including VIEW-RETRACTION-STATE .

  vars N K V W : Cnt .
  var F : Fid .

  rl [derive-first] : clk(N) trigPresent(F) viewFresh(F) qset(V)
      => clk(s(N)) trigPresent(F) viewLive(F) qset(V) current(F, V) .

  --- THE RULING. The trigger is withdrawn, and the derivation goes with it:
  --- `current` is consumed, not left standing. A view holds a statement about
  --- its subject, so a subject that is gone leaves no statement behind.
  rl [retract] : clk(N) trigPresent(F) viewLive(F) current(F, V)
      => clk(s(N)) trigRetracted(F) viewEnded(F) .

  --- Retraction before the view ever derived: same end, nothing to withdraw.
  rl [retract-fresh] : clk(N) trigPresent(F) viewFresh(F)
      => clk(s(N)) trigRetracted(F) viewEnded(F) .

  --- The downstream firing derives from the upstream value that admitted it.
  rl [down-derive] : clk(N) downFresh(V)
      => clk(s(N)) downLive(V) downCurrent(V, V) .

  --- The upstream view supersedes. Its old output is retracted, so under the
  --- ruling the downstream firing keyed by it ends and its derivation is
  --- withdrawn; the new output admits a fresh downstream firing.
  rl [up-supersede] : clk(N) fuel(s(K)) up(V) downLive(V) downCurrent(V, W)
      => clk(s(N)) fuel(K) up(s(V)) downEnded(V) downFresh(s(V)) .
endm

mod WHIPPLESCRIPT-VIEW-PINNED is
  including VIEW-RETRACTION-STATE .

  vars N K V : Cnt .

  rl [down-derive] : clk(N) downFresh(V)
      => clk(s(N)) downLive(V) downCurrent(V, V) .

  --- The counterfactual: pin the downstream firing to its trigger's values, as
  --- a rule firing is pinned. The old firing survives the retraction with its
  --- derivation intact while the new one starts, so two derivations of one
  --- view stand at once and a third arrives on the next upstream move.
  rl [up-supersede-pinned] : clk(N) fuel(s(K)) up(V) downLive(V)
      => clk(s(N)) fuel(K) up(s(V)) downLive(V) downFresh(s(V)) .
endm
